Automata networks, which consist of automata connected in various network configurations, have been studied extensively. A key concern in this field is reachability, which determines if an automaton can reach a specified state. Safety, on the other hand, assesses whether an automaton ever encounters a forbidden state. This paper examines safety for automata networks, particularly those with distinct automata. The main contribution is a method to efficiently solve the safety problem for complex, edge-colored automata networks. This approach is based on a real-time algorithm that reduces the search space significantly. A crucial step involves identifying a "block" within the automaton network, which greatly facilitates searching for safe paths. An in-depth analysis of this block-finding procedure is provided, along with a proof of the algorithm's correctness. Additionally, the paper discusses how to transform a given safety problem into a reachability problem, thereby allowing the use of established tools to solve it. This transformation is achieved by creating a new automaton that represents the automata in the original network, along with a special acceptance condition. Finally, the paper discusses the algorithm's time complexity and how it can be optimized for automata networks. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of this approach, which outperforms current state-of-the-art tools. A detailed description of the implementation is provided, including the code used to run the experiments. This code is available for download from a specified repository. An extension of this work has been published in [1]. A related tool, named ANSIS, is part of the BINDER framework and can be accessed online at the specified URL. 
