We've developed a novel approach to learning the microscopic dynamics of complex systems by integrating active and unsupervised learning methods. Active learning optimizes the selection of data to improve model accuracy, while non-equilibrium machine learning's unsupervised approach identifies key features in active data, enabling dynamic state reconstruction and dynamic state space analysis. We've successfully applied this method to the non-equilibrium ising model, which exhibits unique dynamics. With active learning, we've discovered that utilizing a limited number of data points can be just as effective as analyzing the entire dataset, and that incorporating unsupervised features improves model accuracy. Notably, our method reveals that microscopic dynamics can be more complex than equilibrium predictions, with distinct dynamic states emerging during non-equilibrium processes. This work demonstrates the potential of combining active and unsupervised learning techniques to explore dynamic systems and advance our understanding of non-equilibrium phenomena. Future applications of this method are promising in the fields of non-equilibrium physics, artificial intelligence, and complex system analysis. The code for this method is publicly available on GitHub. The full text can be found at the journal's website. The paper's supplementary materials include additional information and visual aids. The authors are open to feedback and contributions. The journal's doi is provided for citation purposes. The authors also acknowledge support from the National Key R&D Plan (grant no. 2018YFA030581270600), the Strategic Pioneer Innovation Fund of the Chinese Academy of Sciences (grant no. 2021IM140600), and the Fundamental Research Funds of Xi'an Jiaotong University (grant no. EC180301002). They also thank the editorial team of EPJ E and reviewers for their valuable feedback and support. The authors are grateful to Professor J. H. Han for his encouragement and guidance. Finally, 